{% extends "layouts/docs" %}

{% block content %}
	<p>A loader tells Kiln where to find templates. Kiln asks it for a template by name the first time the template is used.</p>

	<h2><code>memoryLoader</code></h2>

	<p>Holds templates in memory, as an object or a <code>Map</code>. It works everywhere, including Cloudflare Workers. A template can be its source or a <a href="/docs/api/precompiling/">precompiled module</a>.</p>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
import { createKiln, memoryLoader } from "@lucidkiln/core";

const kiln = createKiln({
	loader: memoryLoader({
		"layouts/base.kiln": layoutSource,
		"pages/home.kiln": homeSource,
	}),
});
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2><code>fileLoader</code></h2>

	<p>Reads templates from a folder. It is for Node only, so it lives in a separate entry point that never ends up in a Workers bundle.</p>

	<ul>
		<li>It never reads files or folders whose names start with a dot, such as <code>.env</code>.</li>
		<li>It does not follow symlinks below its root, whether they point to files or folders. They are missing templates and are not listed. The root itself may be a symlink.</li>
		<li>A name that points to a folder, or to nothing, is a missing template.</li>
		<li>Other read errors name the template and the error code, such as <code>EACCES</code>, but never the path on disk.</li>
	</ul>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
import { fileLoader } from "@lucidkiln/core/node";

const kiln = createKiln({
	loader: fileLoader({ root: "src/templates" }),
});
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2>Your own loader</h2>

	<p>A loader is an object with a <code>load</code> method. It gets a resolved name, such as <code>pages/home.kiln</code>, and returns the source, a <a href="/docs/api/precompiling/">precompiled module</a> or a <a href="/docs/api/host-components/">host component</a>, or null if the template doesn't exist. It can be async, returning a promise or any other thenable.</p>

	<p>Kiln keeps each template it loads until you call <code>invalidate</code>. It remembers only a limited number of missing names, so names built from requests can't fill memory. A template that fails to load or compile is loaded again the next time it is used.</p>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
import type { Loader } from "@lucidkiln/core";

const kvLoader = (kv: KVNamespace): Loader => ({
	load: (name) => kv.get(`templates/${name}`),
});
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2>Listing templates</h2>

	<p>A loader can also have a <code>list</code> method that returns the name of every template it holds, such as <code>pages/home.kiln</code>. Kiln never calls it while rendering. Tools use it, such as <a href="/docs/api/types/"><code>generateTypes</code></a>.</p>

	<ul>
		<li><code>memoryLoader</code> lists every template you gave it.</li>
		<li><code>fileLoader</code> lists every file under its root, skipping symlinks and files and folders whose names start with a dot. It does not enter skipped folders.</li>
		<li>In your own loader, <code>list</code> is optional and can be async.</li>
	</ul>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
const kvLoader = (kv: KVNamespace): Loader => ({
	load: (name) => kv.get(`templates/${name}`),
	list: async () =>
		(await kv.list({ prefix: "templates/" })).keys.map((key) =>
			key.name.slice("templates/".length),
		),
});
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2>Names</h2>

	<p>Kiln resolves names before it calls the loader. The loader gets a clean path relative to its root, with the <code>.kiln</code> extension added when the name has none. Kiln rejects names that would point above the root. See <a href="/docs/language/syntax/#template-names">Template names</a>.</p>
{% endblock %}
